Falsifies `system_tests::user::suspension_tests::a_suspended_user_thread_stands_still_and_resumes_with_its_registers`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect: `ThreadControlBlock::SUSPEND` forgets its `WRITE` check, so any capability naming a TCB
can stop the thread, a read-only one included. DECISIONS §300 (the paused state, a TCB verb,
silent) makes the TCB capability the whole authority, and the authority is `WRITE` on it, as for
every other method on the object.

Expected to fail: "method 4 obeyed a TCB capability without WRITE". Nothing here is
architecture-specific, so one patch serves every leg.

diff --git a/kernel/src/syscall/thread_control_block.rs b/kernel/src/syscall/thread_control_block.rs
--- a/kernel/src/syscall/thread_control_block.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/syscall/thread_control_block.rs
@@ -49,9 +49,6 @@
             Ok(0)
         }
         abi::thread_control_block::SUSPEND => {
-            if !rights.allows(Rights::WRITE) {
-                return Err(Error::NotPermitted);
-            }
             sched::suspend_thread_control_block(tid)?;
             Ok(0)
         }
